<section class="page-hero">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Conversion Optimization</p>
    <h1>Make the next step easier for your customer.</h1>
    <p class="lead">Find where visitors hesitate or leave, understand the possible causes, and improve the journey from interest to enquiry, booking, or purchase.</p>
    <a class="button" href="/free-marketing-audit/">Get a Free Marketing Audit</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container split">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">When this helps</p>
      <h2>Your traffic needs a path to an outcome.</h2>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p>A page can receive relevant traffic and still make it difficult to act. Confusing messages, unanswered questions, technical issues, and unnecessary form fields can each contribute to friction.</p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Visitors reach important pages but rarely complete the intended action.</li>
        <li>Mobile visitors struggle with navigation, forms, or the booking journey.</li>
        <li>Campaign landing pages do not match the message that brought people there.</li>
        <li>You need to choose which changes to make and how to evaluate them.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">The work</p>
    <h2>Understand the friction before changing the page.</h2>
    <div class="cards">
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Map the journey</h3>
        <p>Define the intended action and review the steps before it. Connect entry pages, calls to action, forms, and confirmation states into one journey.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Inspect the evidence</h3>
        <p>Review funnel data, device differences, page usability, and relevant customer feedback. Distinguish observed issues from hypotheses that need testing.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Prioritize improvements</h3>
        <p>Build a backlog covering messaging, page structure, trust information, forms, and functional defects. Rank changes by likely impact, confidence, and effort.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Evaluate the change</h3>
        <p>Define the primary outcome and review method before implementation. Use controlled tests when traffic supports them, and document limitations when it does not.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container split">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">What you receive</p>
      <h2>Practical changes with a reason behind each one.</h2>
    </div>
    <div>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>A customer journey review with friction points and supporting evidence</li>
        <li>A prioritized backlog of fixes and testable hypotheses</li>
        <li>Recommendations for page content, calls to action, and forms</li>
        <li>An evaluation plan with outcome definitions and relevant safeguards</li>
      </ul>
      <p>For a low-traffic website, a structured usability review and functional fixes may be more useful than an A/B test. Before-and-after observations are reported with context rather than treated as proof of causation.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container split">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Keep the context</p>
      <h2>Review conversion and acquisition together.</h2>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p>A conversion change can reflect a different audience, season, or offer as well as a page improvement. We connect the review with <a href="/services/analytics-growth-strategy/">analytics</a>, <a href="/services/seo-aio/">search intent</a>, and <a href="/services/social-performance-marketing/">campaign activity</a> so the conclusions remain useful.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
